<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<!-- Sounds that are to play quieter than their file is.

     The .wav stays the unaltered source at full resolution and the .ogg is
     produced from it one to one (Tools/encode_sounds.py); the factor belongs
     in the mix, not in the file. Encoding it quieter would also be worse:
     the encoder would get less signal level for the same computational load.

     Anything not listed here plays at 1.0.

     The numbers are measured, not handed down. The shipped .ogg was compared
     against one freshly encoded from the same .wav: same source, same encoder,
     same bitrate, so the difference is exactly the factor set by hand on
     export. Loudness to ITU-R BS.1770. Change the .wav and the factor does
     not change.

     The obvious route - .wav against .ogg - is no good for it: what the
     encoder itself loses in level depends on the frequency and is of the same
     order as the smallest factors here. -->
<Sounds>
	<Sound file="cannon_fire.ogg" volume="0.860" />
	<Sound file="grass.ogg" volume="0.872" />
	<Sound file="push.ogg" volume="0.559" />
	<Sound file="ricochet.ogg" volume="0.458" />
	<Sound file="syringe.ogg" volume="0.914" />
	<Sound file="teleport_begin.ogg" volume="0.666" />
	<Sound file="teleport_end.ogg" volume="0.672" />
	<Sound file="thunder.ogg" volume="0.587" />
</Sounds>
